          <mxGeometry x="30" y="48" width="1200" height="20" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="card1" value="&lt;b&gt;1. Reconciliation only sees order status — not shipments or invoices in Odoo&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Today:&lt;/b&gt; the reconcile poll (&lt;i&gt;integration/erp/infrastructure/odoo_adapter.py: fetch_status&lt;/i&gt;) reads only &lt;i&gt;sale.order.state&lt;/i&gt; and &lt;i&gt;sale.order.invoice_status&lt;/i&gt;. It never reads &lt;i&gt;stock.picking&lt;/i&gt; or &lt;i&gt;account.move&lt;/i&gt;.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why it matters:&lt;/b&gt; a warehouse can validate a delivery directly in Odoo without the sale order's own state/invoice_status changing. The portal has no signal at all — &lt;i&gt;shippedQuantity&lt;/i&gt;/&lt;i&gt;invoiceStatus&lt;/i&gt; stay at zero until an &lt;b&gt;operator manually&lt;/b&gt; calls &lt;i&gt;recordFulfillment&lt;/i&gt;/&lt;i&gt;recordInvoice&lt;/i&gt; — today that's the &lt;b&gt;only&lt;/b&gt; way these facts enter the system.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fix trigger:&lt;/b&gt; an Odoo-side push (Automation Rule on &lt;i&gt;stock.picking&lt;/i&gt; validate / &lt;i&gt;account.move&lt;/i&gt; post) or a dedicated poll of those models, same pattern as the existing order-status webhook." style="fontFamily=Helvetica;rounded=1;arcSize=4;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fillColor=#FFF4E5;strokeColor=#B85450;strokeWidth=1.3;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="90" width="1240" height="160" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="card2" value="&lt;b&gt;2. FIXED (2026-10-04) — Shipment/invoice facts never reached the reseller webhook, and a dead event was still on the list&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Was:&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;DISPATCHABLE_EVENT_TYPES&lt;/i&gt; only had the old lifecycle events, plus a dead &lt;i&gt;OrderFulfilled&lt;/i&gt; (nothing emits it since Increment 5). &lt;i&gt;ShipmentRecorded&lt;/i&gt;/&lt;i&gt;InvoiceRecorded&lt;/i&gt; were missing — and would have been dropped anyway, since &lt;i&gt;Shipment&lt;/i&gt;/&lt;i&gt;Invoice&lt;/i&gt; never stamped a &lt;i&gt;tenant_id&lt;/i&gt;, which the dispatcher requires.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fix:&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;OrderFulfilled&lt;/i&gt; removed; &lt;i&gt;ShipmentRecorded&lt;/i&gt;/&lt;i&gt;InvoiceRecorded&lt;/i&gt; added to both &lt;i&gt;DISPATCHABLE_EVENT_TYPES&lt;/i&gt; and the SNS filter policy (&lt;i&gt;scripts/messaging_bootstrap.py&lt;/i&gt;). &lt;i&gt;Shipment&lt;/i&gt;/&lt;i&gt;Invoice&lt;/i&gt; now carry &lt;i&gt;tenant_id&lt;/i&gt; (threaded from the order, through the GraphQL resolver, the service, and the event itself) so the dispatcher's existing tenant_id guard actually passes." style="fontFamily=Helvetica;rounded=1;arcSize=4;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fillColor=#E8F5E9;strokeColor=#5A9E5A;strokeWidth=1.3;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="262" width="1240" height="150" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="card3" value="&lt;b&gt;3. FIXED (2026-10-04) — A reseller's own order number wasn't enforced unique&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Was:&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;orders.client_reference&lt;/i&gt; had no uniqueness check at all, per-tenant or otherwise.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fix:&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;OrderService.place_order&lt;/i&gt; now checks the projection store (&lt;i&gt;OrderReferenceLookup.exists&lt;/i&gt;) before submitting and raises &lt;i&gt;DuplicateOrderReference&lt;/i&gt; — the common case, no race. Migration 0009 adds &lt;i&gt;UNIQUE(tenant_id, client_reference)&lt;/i&gt; on the DB as the backstop for the narrow race window (two submissions beating each other to the projection); the projector catches that one IntegrityError and logs rather than retrying forever. Verified against live Postgres, not just unit tests." style="fontFamily=Helvetica;rounded=1;arcSize=4;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fillColor=#E8F5E9;strokeColor=#5A9E5A;strokeWidth=1.3;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="422" width="1240" height="120" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="card4" value="&lt;b&gt;4. FIXED (2026-10-04) — Money on the API was a float, not the internal Decimal&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Was:&lt;/b&gt; internally &lt;i&gt;Money&lt;/i&gt;/&lt;i&gt;TaxRate&lt;/i&gt; (&lt;i&gt;src/shared/money.py&lt;/i&gt;) are Decimal-backed and rounded exactly once (&lt;i&gt;round_money&lt;/i&gt;), but &lt;i&gt;MoneyType.amount&lt;/i&gt; in &lt;b&gt;both&lt;/b&gt; GraphQL schemas was declared &lt;i&gt;float&lt;/i&gt; — any client doing its own arithmetic on it would have inherited float rounding error.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fix:&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;MoneyType.amount&lt;/i&gt; is now &lt;i&gt;String!&lt;/i&gt; on both schemas (confirmed via printed SDL), matching the wire-safety pattern already used for &lt;i&gt;quantity&lt;/i&gt; and event payloads. UI's &lt;i&gt;Money&lt;/i&gt; type and &lt;i&gt;formatMoney&lt;/i&gt; updated to parse the string for display only (&lt;i&gt;Number(money.amount)&lt;/i&gt; straight into &lt;i&gt;Intl.NumberFormat&lt;/i&gt;) — the UI never did arithmetic on it, only display, so this closed the gap with zero behavior change for users." style="fontFamily=Helvetica;rounded=1;arcSize=4;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fillColor=#E8F5E9;strokeColor=#5A9E5A;strokeWidth=1.3;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="604" width="1240" height="140" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="card5" value="&lt;b&gt;5. The ordering party is still called &quot;tenant&quot; in the code&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Today:&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;TenantId&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;tenant_id&lt;/i&gt; columns, and &lt;i&gt;Tenant...&lt;/i&gt;-prefixed names run through every module and the DB schema. There is no multi-tenant SaaS concept in this system — a &quot;tenant&quot; &lt;b&gt;is&lt;/b&gt; a reseller, full stop.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Why it matters:&lt;/b&gt; not a functional bug — but an architect reading the code cold will reasonably infer a different kind of system (shared-schema SaaS multi-tenancy) than what this actually is (reseller-scoped B2B ordering). The naming actively misleads about the domain.&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fix trigger:&lt;/b&gt; a rename pass (&lt;i&gt;ResellerId&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;reseller_id&lt;/i&gt;, etc.) — mechanical but wide, since every module touches &lt;i&gt;tenant_id&lt;/i&gt;. Worth doing before onboarding an engineer unfamiliar with this history, cheaper the earlier it's done." style="fontFamily=Helvetica;rounded=1;arcSize=4;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fillColor=#FFF4E5;strokeColor=#B85450;strokeWidth=1.3;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="784" width="1240" height="150" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
